    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d703">

                <pb facs="1005" n="899" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg899"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.00111 <hi rendition="#superscript">Armament
                        Control/Military Secrets</hi>/2189</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Controls</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Green</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-10-09">October 9, 1939</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. William Francis Gibbs, of Gibbs &amp; Cox, Inc., naval architects, called me
                    by telephone this morning. He referred to the Department’s letter of October 3
                    in regard to the proposed construction of vessels of war in this country for the
                    U. S. S. R. and said that he intended to send a copy of that letter to the
                    Soviet Naval Commission.<anchor n="10" xml:id="d703-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Mr. Gibbs added that in view of the present situation in Europe he would have
                    nothing more to do with the project.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph C. Green</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d703-f1" n="10" xml:id="d703-f1-content">
                <p>A copy of the Department’s letter was sent by Gibbs and Cox, Inc., to the Soviet
                    Naval Mission and the Amtorg Trading Corporation, New York, on October 9,
                    1939.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
